  Spd: "4d6"
mdc_base: "4d6x10+40, plus 2d6 per level of experience"
ppe_base: "3d6"
psionics_allowed: false
natural_abilities:
  - { name: "Supernatural Strength and Endurance", description: "P.S. and P.E. are both SUPERNATURAL, which is why a catyr can kill an ordinary human with an accidental slap." }
  - { name: "Invulnerable to Radiation", description: "Their homeworld orbits a binary star and carries radiation levels lethal to most species; the catyr are immune to all of it." }
  - { name: "Ultraviolet Vision", description: "Sees into the ultraviolet range; nightvision 200 feet (61 m)." }
  - { name: "Bio-Regeneration", description: "2D6 M.D.C. per hour." }
  - { name: "Emits Low-Level Radiation", description: "A catyr''s body gives off enough radiation to hurt most normal beings after prolonged exposure. It is why other species keep their distance and why catyr tend to stay in their own communities." }
  - { name: "Horror Factor", description: "10. A number the catyr projects, which the sheet has no field for." }
  - { name: "Average Life Span", description: "200 years." }
  - { name: "Size", description: "Six to seven feet (1.8 to 2.1 m) tall, 160 to 350 lbs (72.5 to 159 kg). Red-skinned humanoids with metallic gold or bronze hair." }
extraction_notes: |
  - Read from the OCR cache of a scan and verified against a 200 dpi render of
    printed 69, which carries the whole stat block. The lore and the Alignment
    line are on printed 68 and both pages were read to the end.
  - CANNOT DEVELOP PSIONICS, stated outright: "Psionic Powers: None. Cannot
    develop psionics." That is `psionics_allowed: false`, which is modelled -
    `rollsForPsionics()` reads it and the wizard''s Race briefing shows it - and
    is not the same as simply having no psionics block, which would let the
    character roll on the Random Psionics Table.
  - A natural M.D.C. creature, so `mdc_base` carries the roll and there is no
    S.D.C. and no CORE_SDC_BY_CLASS entry.
  - MONEY AND CYBERNETICS ARE "Per O.C.C." - the race states neither, so there
    is no `starting_money`. That is the book being explicit rather than silent.
  - A RACE CARRIES NO xp_table. Printed 183 heads the relevant column "Seljuk,
    Noro Mystic Warrior, Kreeghor, and Catyr", which the race shares with three
    others - but composition is race-primary, so a table here would win over the
    occupation''s and silently drop it.
  - THE MAGIC AND PSIONIC O.C.C. RESTRICTION IS PROSE. The book bars O.C.C.s
    "that require psionics or mystic knowledge", and `occ_restrictions` matches
    an `occ_group` - `magic` and `psychic` are two of the five, so a
    `group:magic`/`group:psychic` exclusion would ALMOST work. It is not written
    because the book contradicts it in the next sentence: one-tenth of one
    percent of catyr adventurers do learn magic as a mystic or ley line walker.
    A hard exclusion would make those characters unbuildable, and the rule is a
    rarity rather than a prohibition. It is in the GM Notes.
---

## Lore

The catyr are red-skinned humanoids, a little taller than the average human,
with metallic gold or bronze hair. Their homeworld Catyr-Ra orbits a binary star
and carries radiation that would be lethal to most species. Life thrived there
anyway, the catyr came out on top of it, and they built the technology that took
them to the stars. Like most races of the Three Galaxies they developed science
and not magic.

They met a human planetary collective before the Consortium existed. The first
encounters were violent and a short, savage war was fought over a mineral-rich
planet - then diplomacy carried the day, and the catyr went on to forge
alliances across a number of civilizations. Their homeworld was invited into the
CCW several centuries ago and the population accepted unanimously.

## Feared by their own side

Staunch CCW supporters, and still regarded with some fear. Their bodies emit
enough radiation to hurt a normal being over time, and they are supernaturally
strong and tough enough to kill a human with an accidental punch. So catyr tend
to stay in their own communities, or to become spacers, soldiers and mercenaries
- groups that are feared and shunned in much the same way.

## An open question about where they came from

The catyr temperament is surprisingly human. Their culture, beliefs and
psychology sit so close to humankind''s that some researchers now think the catyr
are genetically altered humans, put on that hostile planet for reasons nobody
knows. The gene-splicers are the usual suspects and there is no evidence for it.

Some catyr have the obvious answer ready: perhaps humans are altered catyr,
seeded across the Megaverse.

## GM Notes

A catyr can take most O.C.C.s. The book bars those requiring psionics or mystic
knowledge, then immediately notes that about one-tenth of one percent of catyr
adventurers learn magic as a mystic or a ley line walker - so treat it as a
rarity rather than a wall. Common choices are spacer, runner, CAF trooper or
officer, pirate, tracer and the other men-at-arms, plus the adventurer and
scholar trades.

Money and cybernetics both come from the O.C.C.; the race states neither.
', 'published', 'data-script'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'catyr');


-- Read the result back rather than trusting the exit code. d1-apply prints
-- these, and a CR in the stored markdown means the checkout mangled the file.
SELECT class_id, name, status, length(markdown) AS bytes, instr(markdown, char(13)) > 0 AS has_cr
  FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'catyr';

